# One line ending everywhere, LF, in the index AND in the working tree.
#
# This exists because of a real failure rather than a style preference. With `core.autocrlf=true`
# and no .gitattributes, a file is stored LF and checked out CRLF, so what a Windows contributor
# runs is not byte-identical to what is committed. `scripts/check-advisories.mjs` began with a
# shebang, and the CRLF checkout of that file made vitest fail to import it with
# "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token" while the same file passed on Linux and had passed
# locally before the commit. The tests that were a commit's evidence could not run on the platform
# they were written on, and CI was green, which is the worst possible arrangement of those two
# facts.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF on every platform. The index here was
# already LF, so `git add --renormalize .` produced no changes at all: this governs what a FRESH
# clone gets, which is what CI and anyone reading this repository will have. An existing working
# tree keeps its CRLF files until they are next checked out, and that is fine. Do not try to fix
# that with `git checkout-index --force`: it overwrites every tracked file from the index and
# throws away uncommitted work, which is how this line came to be written twice.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to translate anything inside them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
